{%- comment -%}
  Publishes the National Gym Service option allowlist to the PDP as JSON so the
  price logic in product-custom-options.js can tell which selected options are
  billed by National Gym Service LLC rather than collected by Fitness
  Superstore at checkout.

  This reads the SAME shop metafield the Cart Transform function reads
  (`fs_bundle.ngs_allowlist`), so the storefront and the charge logic can never
  disagree about which options are deferred. If the metafield is absent the
  snippet emits nothing, the script finds no allowlist, and every option is
  totalled exactly as it is today.

  Entries are keyed by bare numeric variant id because that is what the option
  inputs carry in the DOM. The function keys on the full GID.

  THE SIZE GATE, AND WHY THE THEME NEEDS ONE AT ALL

  Shopify SILENTLY omits a metafield value over 10,000 bytes from a FUNCTION's
  input — it arrives as `null`, with no error. Liquid has no such limit, so
  this snippet would happily read a value the function cannot see.

  That asymmetry is the dangerous one. Without this gate an oversized allowlist
  makes the function charge every NGS service in full while this page tells the
  customer "Not charged today" — the page promising a deferral the checkout
  will not honour. Revenue is safe; the customer is misled, and National Gym
  Service may be told to invoice an amount Fitness Superstore already collected.

  So the theme applies the function's own limit to itself: over 10,000 bytes,
  emit nothing and display exactly as today. The generator enforces a tighter
  9,000-byte authoring margin, so reaching this gate means something upstream
  went wrong and silence is the correct response.

  The size is measured on the re-serialized value. The stored value is written
  compact by scripts/rekey-ngs-allowlist-to-staging.mjs and is pure ASCII, so
  re-serializing reproduces it closely enough for a safety net that should never
  be the operative path.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign ngs_allowlist = shop.metafields.fs_bundle.ngs_allowlist.value -%}
{%- assign ngs_raw_size = ngs_allowlist | json | size -%}

{%- if ngs_allowlist.options.size > 0 and ngs_raw_size <= 10000 -%}
  <script type="application/json" data-ngs-allowlist>
    {
      "deferralActive": {% if ngs_allowlist.deferralActive %}true{% else %}false{% endif %},
      "options": [
        {%- comment -%}
          v2 is the compact tuple [variantId, priceMinorUnits, modeCode], which
          is what makes the allowlist fit at all: the verbose v1 object form put
          171 rows at ~30 kB against a 10 kB ceiling. v1 is still read because
          it is what the 25 September preview wrote.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if ngs_allowlist.version == 2 -%}
          {%- for entry in ngs_allowlist.options -%}
            {%- assign mode_code = entry[2] -%}
            {%- if mode_code == 1 -%}
              {%- assign mode_name = 'quote_required' -%}
            {%- else -%}
              {%- assign mode_name = 'defer' -%}
            {%- endif -%}
            {%- assign price_major = entry[1] | divided_by: 100.0 -%}
            {"id":{{ entry[0] | json }},"mode":{{ mode_name | json }},"price":{{ price_major }}}
            {%- unless forloop.last -%},{%- endunless -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
        {%- else -%}
          {%- for entry in ngs_allowlist.options -%}
            {%- assign numeric_id = entry.variantId | split: '/' | last -%}
            {"id":{{ numeric_id | json }},"mode":{{ entry.mode | json }},"price":{{ entry.price | default: 0 }}}
            {%- unless forloop.last -%},{%- endunless -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      ]
    }
  </script>
{%- endif -%}
